and a pair of B4. Discarding B1 keeps both pairs intact.\n\nAlso, looking at opponents' discards and melds:  \n- Next player has a concealed kong and melds with terminals (B7-B9, B1 pung) \u2014 likely going for a hand with terminals/honors or all one suit?  \n- Across player has only one chow.  \n- Prevailing wind is West (F3), seat wind is South (F2) \u2014 I have no wind pungs.  \n- Many terminals/honors have been discarded, so keeping B1 seems less useful.\n\n**Reason:**  \nDiscarding **B1** is the safest and most strategic because:  \n1. It\u2019s a terminal with very few left (1 unseen), making it hard to complete a sequence or pair.  \n2. It preserves both my natural pairs (B4 and J2), which are closer to forming a winning pair.  \n3. It moves me toward **All Simples** (a 2-point pattern) if I eventually discard J2 as well, but keeping J2 for now retains Dragon Pung chance and pair flexibility.  \n4. No immediate risk: B1 is not a dangerous discard given history (previous player discarded B1, next player punged B1, so it\u2019s relatively safe).\n\n**Answer:** Play B1"}
